@* The one way every page says how something the person just did turned out: green with a check for success, red for
   an error. One per page, placed near the top of the page's content; it sticks to the top of the window while the
   page scrolls, so the outcome is seen even when the button was far down. It stays until dismissed or replaced
   (nothing times out, so nobody misses it).

   The live region is always rendered, so a screen reader announces a notice added to an interactive page; an error
   is also an alert. Interactive pages pass OnDismiss. Static pages (form posts) don't: their close button is hidden
   until app.js shows it and handles it, so without JavaScript there is simply no close button. See docs/design.md,
   "Web UI". *@

<div class="notice-region" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
    @if (Value is not null)
    {
        <div @key="Value" id="notice" class="notice notice-@(Value.IsError ? "error" : "success")" role="@(Value.IsError ? "alert" : null)">
            <span class="notice-icon" aria-hidden="true">@(Value.IsError ? "!" : "✓")</span>
            <span class="notice-text">@Value.Text</span>
            @if (OnDismiss.HasDelegate)
            {
                <button type="button" class="notice-dismiss" aria-label="Dismiss" @onclick="OnDismiss">×</button>
            }
            else
            {
                <button type="button" class="notice-dismiss" aria-label="Dismiss" data-notice-dismiss hidden>×</button>
            }
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The notice to show, or null for none.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Notice? Value { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Clears the notice on an interactive page (set it to null).</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnDismiss { get; set; }
}
